  "termsAgreeBox": "I have read and agree to the Terms of Use and Disclaimer",
  "termsAccept": "Accept",
  "termsDecline": "Decline",
  "termsDeclinedTitle": "DNet stays closed",
  "termsDeclinedBody": "You declined the Terms of Use, so DNet won't start. Nothing has been opened, sent or changed. You can read the terms again, or quit.",
  "termsReadAgain": "Read the terms again",
  "termsQuit": "Quit DNet",
  "termsAcceptedOn": "You accepted version {version} on {date}.",
  "@termsAcceptedOn": {"placeholders": {"version": {"type": "int"}, "date": {"type": "DateTime", "format": "yMMMMd"}}},
  "termsDocIntro": "These Terms of Use and Disclaimer (\"terms\") are an agreement between you and the authors and contributors of DNet (together, \"the authors\"). They apply to your use of the DNet app and of everything it does on your device (\"DNet\"). Please read all of them. They limit the authors' responsibility to you, and they make you responsible for how you use DNet.\n\nYou must accept these terms before DNet can be used. If you do not agree with them, choose Decline and do not use DNet.",
  "termsDoc1Title": "What DNet is",
  "termsDoc1Body": "DNet is free, open-source software for sending messages, calls and files directly between devices without the internet. Devices reach each other over Bluetooth, over Wi-Fi on the same local network, over Wi-Fi Direct and, if you connect one, through an external LoRa radio. When the person you want is out of range, encrypted messages may be passed along by other DNet devices.\n\nThe authors do not run any server, network or messaging service for DNet. They do not carry, store, see, check or control any message, call, file, name, location or other content sent with it, and they cannot do so. DNet is a tool that runs on your device and on the devices of the people you talk to.",
  "termsDoc2Title": "Accepting these terms",
  "termsDoc2Body": "By ticking \"I have read and agree\" and choosing Accept, you confirm that you have read and understood these terms and agree to be bound by them. DNet keeps a record on your device of which version of the terms you accepted, and when. If you do not accept them, DNet will not open.\n\nIf you use DNet for or on behalf of an organisation, you confirm that you have the authority to accept these terms for it, and \"you\" then includes that organisation.",
  "termsDoc3Title": "Who may use DNet",
  "termsDoc3Body": "You may use DNet only if you are old enough, under the law that applies to you, to use communication services like it and to accept these terms yourself. If you are below that age, you may use DNet only with the permission and supervision of a parent or legal guardian, who accepts these terms for you and is responsible for your use.\n\nYou may not use DNet where the law that applies to you forbids it, or if you have been barred from using software like it.",
  "termsDoc4Title": "You are responsible for lawful use",
  "termsDoc4Body": "You alone are responsible for how you use DNet, and for making sure that your use is lawful where you are and wherever your messages or radio signals reach. Laws differ from country to country and change over time. Among other things, you are responsible for following the laws and rules on:\n• Encryption: some countries restrict the use, import or export of encryption software.\n• Radio: if you connect a LoRa or other radio, you must choose only a frequency band that is allowed where you are, and keep to its rules on transmit power, antennas, duty cycle or airtime, channel use, and any licence, registration or certification. DNet's region choices and airtime limits are only an aid. They are not legal advice and may be wrong, incomplete or out of date.\n• Bluetooth, Wi-Fi and Wi-Fi Direct: where and when wireless devices may be used, including on aircraft, in hospitals and at secure or restricted sites.\n• Recording and capture: recording, saving or sharing calls, voices, screens, screenshots, photos or the location of other people may need their consent and may be restricted.\n• Content: everything you send, share or publish with DNet, including in spaces and public channels, and any rights that others have in it.\n• Privacy and data protection: any duties you have when you collect, keep or share information about other people.\n\nNothing in DNet, its documentation or these terms is legal advice. If you are unsure whether something is allowed where you are, find out before you do it.",
  "termsDoc5Title": "Things you must not do",
  "termsDoc5Body": "You agree not to use DNet, or help or allow anyone else to use it, to:\n• break any law or regulation, or encourage or help anyone else to break one;\n• create, send, keep or share illegal content, including content that sexually exploits or endangers children, incites violence, hatred or terrorism, or is fraudulent;\n• harass, threaten, stalk, bully, intimidate, impersonate or deceive anyone, or send unwanted messages in bulk;\n• infringe anyone's copyright, trademark, privacy, publicity or other rights, or share another person's private information or images without the right to;\n• track, watch or locate a person without their knowledge and consent;\n• interfere with, disrupt or overload any network, device, service or radio frequency, including by transmitting where, when, at a power or for longer than is allowed, or by deliberately causing interference to other radio users;\n• get into any device, account or data without permission, or spread malware;\n• make identities in bulk, or get around DNet's protections, such as the identity stamp, chat requests, blocking or the app lock, to reach or harm other people;\n• plan or carry out anything that could cause death, injury, or damage to property or the environment.\n\nThe authors cannot see how DNet is used and do not monitor it, but none of these uses is allowed.",
  "termsDoc6Title": "Not for emergencies or critical use",
  "termsDoc6Body": "DNet is not an emergency service and cannot contact emergency services. Do not rely on it for emergency, life-safety or other critical communication: not as your only way to call for help, not to coordinate rescue or medical care, and not for anything where a failure, delay or loss of a message could lead to death, injury, or serious damage to property or the environment. This includes medical, aviation, maritime, vehicle, industrial-control and disaster-response use. Always keep another way to reach help.",
  "termsDoc7Title": "No promise that messages arrive or stay private",
  "termsDoc7Body": "DNet works only when other devices are within range and the links between them happen to work. Messages, calls and files may be delayed, arrive out of order, arrive more than once, arrive incomplete or not arrive at all, and DNet may not be able to tell you which. A message shown as sent, relayed or delivered may still not have been read.\n\nDNet is designed to protect what you send with end-to-end encryption, but no software is perfect. The authors do not promise that DNet is secure, that it will keep your messages, calls, files, identity or location private, or that it will hide the fact that you use it. People near you may be able to tell that your device is sending radio signals, and anyone listening on a LoRa band can see that two DNet radios are talking, when, and how much. Known limits are listed in SECURITY.md in the source code.\n\nOnce you send something, the people who receive it can keep, copy, record, photograph or pass it on, and neither you nor the authors can take it back. Disappearing messages, view-once media and deleting a message depend on the other person's device, and cannot stop them from keeping a copy.",
  "termsDoc8Title": "Early software, not independently audited",
  "termsDoc8Body": "DNet is early-stage software. It has had an internal security review but no independent security audit, and its Bluetooth, Wi-Fi Direct, mesh relay, NFC, screen sharing and LoRa radio features have not yet been verified between real devices. It may have bugs and security flaws. It may lose or damage data, use a lot of battery or other resources, behave differently on different devices, and change or stop working without notice. Do not rely on it to protect anyone, and keep your own copies of anything important.",
  "termsDoc9Title": "Your data stays with you",
  "termsDoc9Body": "The authors run no servers and collect no data. DNet has no accounts, analytics, advertising or tracking, and it sends nothing to the authors. Your identity, messages, contacts and settings are kept encrypted on your device, together with the record of the terms you accepted.\n\nUsing DNet means your device exchanges information with other devices, directly or through other DNet devices: for example the name you choose, your public key, and, when you choose to send them, messages, files, photos, your status or your location. Your device may also pass on other people's sealed messages, which it cannot read; you can turn this off in Privacy. What other people receive is on their devices, outside your control and outside the authors' control.\n\nYou are responsible for your device and its security, for keeping DNet and your system up to date, for your app lock passcode, and for keeping copies of anything you need. If you lose your device, forget your passcode or wipe DNet, your data may be gone for good: the authors hold no copy and cannot recover it.",
  "termsDoc10Title": "Other people, devices and links",
  "termsDoc10Body": "DNet puts you in touch with people the authors do not know. The authors are not responsible for other users, their content or their conduct, or for what they do with what you send them. Content you receive may be false, offensive, harmful or illegal, and the authors do not review it. Think before you accept a chat request or verify someone, and use Decline and Block when you need to.\n\nRadios, boards, firmware and other hardware or software made by others, such as Meshtastic devices, are not made or supported by the authors. Their makers' terms, and the law where you use them, apply to them.\n\nDNet never opens a link by itself. If you choose to open a link, it opens in your own browser, outside DNet, and the authors are not responsible for the site or for what happens there.",
  "termsDoc11Title": "No warranty",
  "termsDoc11Body": "DNET IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\" AND \"AS AVAILABLE\", WITH ALL FAULTS AND WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND. To the fullest extent the law allows, the authors disclaim all warranties, conditions and terms, whether express, implied or statutory, including any of merchantability, satisfactory quality, fitness for a particular purpose, title, non-infringement, accuracy, security, availability and quiet enjoyment, and any arising from a course of dealing or usage of trade.\n\nThe authors do not warrant that DNet will meet your needs, that it will work without interruption or error, that it is secure or free of harmful components, that defects will be fixed, or that it will work with any particular device, radio or operating system. No advice or information, spoken or written, that you get from the authors or from anywhere else creates any warranty. You use DNet entirely at your own risk.",
  "termsDoc12Title": "Limitation of liability",
  "termsDoc12Body": "TO THE FULLEST EXTENT THE LAW ALLOWS, IN NO EVENT WILL THE AUTHORS, CONTRIBUTORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE to you or to anyone else for any direct, indirect, incidental, special, consequential, exemplary or punitive damages, or for any loss of data, messages, privacy, profits, revenue, goodwill, business or opportunity, any damage to devices or equipment, any personal injury or death, or the cost of any substitute service, arising out of or in connection with DNet or these terms, however caused and on any theory of liability, whether in contract, tort (including negligence), strict liability or otherwise, even if they were told that such damage was possible. This includes anything done, sent or not done by other people using DNet.\n\nWhere the law does not allow liability to be excluded completely, the authors' total liability is limited to the smallest amount the law allows. DNet is given away free of charge, and you agree that these limits are a fair basis for that.\n\nSome places do not allow certain warranties to be excluded or certain liability to be limited, such as liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence, or for fraud. There, the exclusions and limits in these terms apply only as far as the law allows, and nothing in these terms takes away any right you have that cannot be changed by agreement.",
  "termsDoc13Title": "Indemnity",
  "termsDoc13Body": "To the fullest extent the law allows, you agree to defend, indemnify and hold harmless the authors and contributors against any claim, demand, action, loss, liability, damage, fine, penalty, cost or expense, including reasonable legal fees, that arises from or relates to: your use of DNet; anything you send, share or publish with it; your breach of these terms; your breach of any law or regulation, including rules on radio and encryption; or your infringement of anyone else's rights.",
  "termsDoc14Title": "The open-source licence",
  "termsDoc14Body": "DNet's source code is published under the MIT License, which you can read in the app under About and in the LICENSE file of the source code. These terms cover your use of DNet. They do not take away any right that the licence gives you to use, copy, change or share the source code, and the licence's own disclaimer of warranty and limitation of liability apply as well as these terms.\n\nIf you change DNet or share a copy of it, changed or not, you are responsible for that copy, and you must not suggest that the authors made, checked or approve of your version. Software and fonts that DNet includes from others come under their own licences, listed in About under Open-source licences.",
  "termsDoc15Title": "Changes to DNet and to these terms",
  "termsDoc15Body": "The authors may change, pause or stop work on DNet at any time without notice, and they have no obligation to provide updates, fixes or support.\n\nThe authors may change these terms. When a later version of DNet comes with changed terms, it asks you to read and accept them before you can go on using it. If you do not accept the changed terms, stop using DNet. The version of the terms you accepted, and when, is kept on your device and shown in About.",
  "termsDoc16Title": "Stopping use",
  "termsDoc16Body": "You can stop using DNet at any time: wipe its data from Privacy and remove the app. If you break these terms, you are no longer allowed to use DNet. The sections on lawful use, things you must not do, no warranty, limitation of liability and indemnity, and these general terms, keep applying to anything that happened before you stopped.",
  "termsDoc17Title": "General terms",
  "termsDoc17Body": "If any part of these terms is found invalid or unenforceable, it is limited or changed only as far as needed to make it enforceable, and the rest of the terms remain in full force.\n\nIf the authors do not enforce any part of these terms, that does not mean they give up the right to do so later.\n\nThese terms, together with the open-source licence, are the whole agreement between you and the authors about DNet, and they replace anything said before. Every author and contributor may rely on them. You may not transfer your rights or duties under them to anyone else.\n\nHeadings are there to help you read. They do not change what the terms mean.\n\nQuestions about DNet or these terms can be raised on the project's GitHub page, linked from About. Security problems should be reported privately, as SECURITY.md in the source code describes.",
  "termsDocClosing": "By ticking \"I have read and agree\" and choosing Accept, you confirm that you have read these terms to the end, that you understand them, that you are allowed to accept them, and that you agree to them.",
  "aboutTagline": "Your offline connector",
  "aboutVersion": "Version {version}",
